---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

/* EVERY CLAIM ABOUT AITUM ON THIS PAGE TRACES TO docs/notes/competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md,
 * section 4, and to nothing else. That file is the only sanctioned source, and
 * the reason is the paragraph it opens with:
 *
 *   Aitum Multistream DOES send different audio to different destinations.
 *
 * It assigns an OBS audio track per output, and the summing that produced those
 * tracks happened upstream in OBS's Advanced Audio Properties. So "Aitum cannot
 * do per-destination audio" is FALSE and must never appear here in any wording.
 * The true argument is narrower and better, and it is the one this page makes:
 * they select a mix OBS has already built, so N different mixes means N track
 * layouts maintained in OBS and N encodes on the stream PC.
 *
 * That is also why this page is a WALKTHROUGH rather than a table. The
 * difference is not a capability either product lacks -- it is how much work the
 * same finished result costs, and a Yes/No grid cannot show that. It would also
 * have made this page a near-copy of /vs/obs-multi-rtmp, whose argument is about
 * the uplink rather than the mixing desk.
 */

/* The same job, done twice. Deliberately the modest version of the task -- two
 * destinations, one difference between them -- because the honest thing about
 * Aitum is that at this size it is fine, and the cost only compounds later. */
const steps = [
  {
    tool: "In OBS, with Aitum Multistream",
    where: "Every step is on the stream PC.",
    list: [
      "Open Advanced Audio Properties and build the layout: mic and game on Track 1, mic and game and music on Track 2. This is the mix, and this is where it is made.",
      "Add the Twitch destination in Aitum, set its audio track to 2.",
      "Add the YouTube destination, set its audio track to 1.",
      "Choose an encoder for each destination, or clone the main OBS output for both.",
      "Both destinations now upload from this machine.",
    ],
  },
  {
    tool: "In polyemesis",
    where: "One upload leaves the stream PC. The rest happens on the server.",
    list: [
      "In OBS, put each source on its own track — mic on 1, game on 2, music on 3. No layout to design, because nothing is being summed yet.",
      "Send one multitrack SRT stream to polyemesis.",
      "Tick the tracks each destination should hear: Twitch gets 1, 2 and 3; YouTube gets 1 and 2.",
      "Change your mind mid-broadcast by unticking a box. The track layout in OBS never moves.",
    ],
  },
];

/* Stated because a comparison page that finds no merit in the other tool reads
 * as marketing, and this audience checks. Every line traces to section 4. */
const merits = [
  {
    h: "Free, and GPL-2.0",
    p: "The Multistream plugin costs nothing. Aitum sells a separate desktop automation suite at $4.99/mo, and Multistream does not require it.",
  },
  {
    h: "Per-destination encoding",
    p: "Each destination can clone the main OBS output or re-encode independently, with its own resolution, bitrate and encoder. That is not a lesser feature than what a server does — it is the same feature, paid for locally.",
  },
  {
    h: "It is where you already are",
    p: "It runs inside OBS. There is no server to provision, no second machine, no ingest to point anywhere. For a great many broadcasts that is the correct architecture and this page is not an argument against it.",
  },
  {
    h: "StreamSuite",
    p: "Multistream is now bundled with Aitum Vertical under the StreamSuite name, with unified multi-chat and metadata panels alongside it.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Aitum Multistream vs polyemesis: the audio model"
  description="The Aitum Multistream OBS plugin assigns one OBS audio track per destination. polyemesis sums tracks server-side. Both free — here is what differs."
  path="/vs/aitum-multistream"
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    crumbs={[
      { label: "polyemesis", href: "/" },
      { label: "Comparison", href: "/comparison" },
      { label: "Aitum Multistream", },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="wrap py-12 sm:py-16">
    <p class="eyebrow">Compared</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Aitum Multistream picks a mix. polyemesis builds one.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      The Aitum Multistream OBS plugin can already send different audio to
      different platforms — anyone telling you otherwise has not used it. Each
      destination is assigned one of OBS's six audio tracks, and that track goes
      out. The question this page answers is a narrower one: where the mix on
      that track gets made, and what it costs you to have more than one.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl rounded-token border-l-2 border-primary bg-surface/60 py-4 pl-5 pr-4">
      <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
        <strong>The difference in one sentence:</strong> Aitum selects a mix OBS
        has already built, so three different mixes means three track layouts
        maintained in Advanced Audio Properties and each output encoded on the
        stream PC. polyemesis sums the tracks server-side, so one upload and one
        video encode feed every destination.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------ the walkthrough -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">The same broadcast, set up both ways</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Two destinations. Twitch takes the music, YouTube does not. Both tools
        finish this job, and comparing the routes is more useful than comparing
        a column of Yes against a column of No.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
        {
          steps.map((s) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <p class="font-medium text-fg">{s.tool}</p>
              <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] text-subtle">{s.where}</p>
              <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                {s.list.map((step, i) => (
                  <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
                    <span class="mt-px font-mono text-[11px] text-primary tnum">{i + 1}</span>
                    <span class="text-pretty">{step}</span>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        At two destinations these are the same amount of work, and the honest
        summary is that it does not matter which you pick. The divergence starts
        at the third: a new mix in Aitum is a new track layout in OBS, and OBS
        has six tracks. In polyemesis a new mix is a row of checkboxes, and the
        thing being ticked is the ingest, which has not changed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------- what it costs -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Where the work lands</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Not a capability difference. A location difference — and on a machine
        that is also running the game, location is the whole argument.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid max-w-4xl gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-subtle">
            Aitum Multistream
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
            The mixing desk is OBS's Advanced Audio Properties. The encoders run
            on the stream PC, one per destination unless you clone the main
            output. Every destination uploads from your connection.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">
            polyemesis
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
            The mixing desk is the server, with a gain per input-channel-to-output-channel
            cell and a loudness target measured after the routing. One upload
            leaves the stream PC; the picture is copied through rather than
            re-encoded.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
        The cost belongs here too, because it is real: mixing means decoding the
        audio and encoding it again for every destination, where selecting a
        track is a copy. polyemesis also needs a server, and Aitum does not. If
        one mix suits everyone, the plugin is free and you should use it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------- their merits -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What Aitum Multistream does well</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Checked against Aitum's own repository and site on
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-fg">15 August 2026</span>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          merits.map((m) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <p class="font-medium text-fg">{m.h}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{m.p}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------ pick the other -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Use Aitum, not this</h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 max-w-3xl space-y-3">
        {
          [
            "One mix suits every platform. This is most broadcasts, and adding a server to serve it is a worse setup, not a better one.",
            "Your track layout is settled and you are not editing it under load.",
            "You want a destination at a different resolution or bitrate and your PC has the headroom to encode it. Aitum does this natively.",
            "You do not want to run, patch or pay for a server. That is a legitimate position and there is no answer to it here.",
          ].map((l) => (
            <li class="flex gap-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1 w-1 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-subtle" aria-hidden="true" />
              {l}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        polyemesis is worth the server when the mixes outnumber the tracks, when
        the stream PC has no headroom left to encode with, or when the upload is
        the ceiling — one stream out instead of one per platform.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap py-20 text-center" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">Weighing up the others too?</h2>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/comparison" class="btn btn-ghost">All five compared</a>
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install polyemesis</a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-subtle">
      Also compared:
      <a href="/vs/obs-multi-rtmp" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">obs-multi-rtmp</a>
      ·
      <a href="/vs/streamelements" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">StreamElements</a>
      ·
      <a href="/vs/restreamer" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Restreamer and restream.io</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
